 The console's serial mirror, driven through `handle-console-input` and
 read back off the wire.

 THE TWO CHANNELS ARE THE SAME PORT AND THAT IS THE POINT. This test's own
 `print-line-uni` and the console's mirror both end at 1016, so a mirrored
 line lands in the harness output interleaved with the test's own. The
 `cmd:` and `status:` lines below were not printed by this chapter; the
 console put them there. Nothing else in this test could produce them.

 MIRRORING IS OFF UNTIL IT IS TURNED ON, and the first block proves it
 rather than assuming it. A command is run with the flag clear and only
 the test's own line appears; the same shape after `serial.on` produces
 two more. Without the first half, a mirror that was hardwired on would
 pass -- and it would also mean every existing console test had started
 writing to the wire, which is exactly the regression this ordering
 catches.

 `serial.on` MIRRORS ITSELF, because the flag is read from the state the
 command produced rather than the state it was given. That is asserted
 here because the opposite is the more natural way to write it and fails
 silently in the one case anybody checks first.

 `serial.off` ALSO MIRRORS ITSELF, for the opposite reason: the echo is
 armed by the flag before the command as well as the one after it, so the
 last thing a far-end reader sees is the console saying it is going quiet.
 Without that line the stream just stops, and a mirror that was switched
 off is indistinguishable from a console that has hung -- the one question
 this module exists to answer. Both directions are asserted below, and the
 flag values are printed either side, because the flag flipping and the
 echo firing are separate failures: a `__record-set` that silently did not
 write left the status line reading `serial mirror ON` above a wire with
 nothing on it, which is how that defect was found.

 THE INBOUND CONVERSION IS TESTED AS TEXT, not through a port. Bytes
 arriving from COM1 are ASCII and Text is CCE, so `sb-append-byte` has to
 convert; if it did not, a remote `mem` would build a Text that is not
 equal to the `mem` the dispatcher matches on, and remote commands would
 never fire while nothing reported an error. Comparing the assembled line
 against a literal is what discriminates that, and it needs no serial
 input at all.

 We say:

Section: Fixtures

  bare-console : UefiConsoleState
  bare-console = UefiConsoleState {
    sys-table = 0,
    con-out = 0,
    con-in = 0,
    rows = 25,
    cols = 80,
    cursor-row = 0,
    cursor-col = 0
  }

  bare-disk : DiskFactStore
  bare-disk = DiskFactStore { superblock = empty-superblock, mem = fact-store-empty, base = 0, limit = 0 }

  bare-keys : Keypair
  bare-keys = generate-keypair (sha256 (text-to-bytes "codex-serial-mirror-test"))

Section: Reporting

  report : Text, DevConsoleState -> [Console] Nothing
  report (label) (s) = act
    print-line-uni (label & ": " & s.status)
  end

Section: Assembling From Bytes

  feed : Text, List Integer, Integer -> Text
  feed (acc) (bytes) (i) =
    if i >= list-length bytes then acc
    else feed (sb-append-byte acc (list-at bytes i)) bytes (i + 1)

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, Device.Port, Device.Block] Nothing = act
    let s0 = dev-console-init bare-console bare-disk bare-keys
    in let d0 = dispatch-main-menu "debug" s0
    in act

      print-line-uni "-- mirror off --"
      q <- handle-console-input d0 "perf"
      report "quiet" q

      print-line-uni "-- turning it on --"
      m <- handle-console-input q "serial.on"
      report "armed" m
      print-line-uni ("flag-after-on: " & show (m.debugger.serial-mirror))

      print-line-uni "-- mirror on --"
      p <- handle-console-input m "idt.counts"
      report "mirrored" p

      print-line-uni "-- turning it off --"
      f <- handle-console-input p "serial.off"
      report "disarmed" f
      print-line-uni ("flag-after-off: " & show (f.debugger.serial-mirror))

      print-line-uni "-- mirror off again --"
      z <- handle-console-input f "perf"
      report "quiet-again" z

      let line = feed "" [109, 101, 109] 0
      in print-line-uni ("assembled: [" & line & "] matches-literal: " & show (line == "mem"))

      let dropped = feed "" [109, 7, 101, 109] 0
      in print-line-uni ("control-dropped: [" & dropped & "] matches-literal: " & show (dropped == "mem"))

      print-line-uni ("remote-default-off: " & show (z.debugger.serial-remote))
    end
  end
